a.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Hordron_Edge_stone_circle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1365389.jpg/960px-Hordron_Edge_stone_circle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1365389.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Hordron_Edge_stone_circle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1365389.jpg/500px-Hordron_Edge_stone_circle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1365389.jpg","caption":"Hordron Edge stone circle","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hordron_Edge_stone_circle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1365389.jpg","ok_print":false},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Highlow_Hall%2C_Derbyshire.jpg/960px-Highlow_Hall%2C_Derbyshire.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Highlow_Hall%2C_Derbyshire.jpg/500px-Highlow_Hall%2C_Derbyshire.jpg","caption":"Highlow Hall","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Highlow_Hall,_Derbyshire.jpg","ok_print":false}],"intro":{"body":"Hathersage is in the Peak District of Derbyshire. Hathersage is a tourist destination because of the scenery of the Hope and Derwent valleys, its literary connections, and easy access by train or road from Sheffield and Manchester. Its visitors come to swim (open-air heated swimming pool, with cafe open all year), climb (Stanage Edge, which with other nearby edges have been the nursery for many famous British rock and mountain climbers) or ramble in its river valleys or hillwalk on its open moors. Hathersage Moor is the site of the Carl Wark hillfort and Higger Tor; both are now within the Sheffield boundary.","grounding":[{"source":"wikivoyage","title":"Hathersage","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Hathersage","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"short":"Hathersage is a small village inside the Peak District National Park. Kinder Scout and Lose Hill are the walks people come for, Ladybower Reservoir is close, and Peveril Castle a drive off, so allow four hours between June and August.","short_src":"rewrite","facts":{"population":2018,"visit_h":3.6}},"highlights":[{"i